From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: slothfulchat <patches@slothfulchat.invalid>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 12:23:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 81/86] web-app: long-press context menus and touch reordering
 on phones

Long-pressing a profile in the account sidebar did nothing on any
phone, for different reasons per platform.

Android: the <li> around each account is `draggable` for the desktop
reorder gesture, and Blink spends a touch long press on starting that
drag (which no finger can complete) instead of firing `contextmenu`.
The same combination breaks the gallery media items, the gallery file
icon and the message attachment icon; all four now switch `draggable`
off on coarse pointers via one isCoarsePointer() helper.

iOS: two more layers. The press selected the text under the finger --
the app-wide `user-select: none` on button is only honored prefixed,
so -webkit-user-select and -webkit-touch-callout now sit beside it.
And Safari fires no `contextmenu` event for touch presses at all since
iOS 13 (mdn/browser-compat-data#6376), so a document-level fallback
synthesizes one once a single touch has rested within an 8px slop for
700ms. Per-press feature detection, no UA sniffing: a browser that
fires its own event (Blink, at ~500ms) cancels the fallback for that
press. Editable text is left alone (a long press there means
cursor/paste), and so is explicitly selectable text -- message bodies
carry `user-select: text`, and a long press there means "select";
a menu on top would make selecting text on iOS impossible, so a
message's menu opens from the bubble's non-text parts instead
(`user-select: auto` is resolved by walking up to the nearest
text/none, like the browser does). The synthesized menu's release
click is swallowed -- iOS, unlike Android, still delivers it, straight
onto the menu's backdrop, closing what it just opened.

Turning `draggable` off on touch would leave phones unable to reorder,
so the sidebar drives the gesture itself from touch events: moving
right away scrolls, resting opens the context menu (which puts the
picked-up profile back down), holding for 250ms and then moving
reorders -- taken over from the scroller via preventDefault on a
non-passive touchmove. The mouse path keeps the browser's drag and
drop; both paths end in one pure reorderedAccounts() with mocha cases.
scripts/test-account-reorder-touch.mjs (outer repo) drives real CDP
touch sequences at the real hook and fallback in chromium -- which,
like iOS, fires no native long-press contextmenu for them: hold-then-
move reorders, an early move scrolls, a rest opens the menu, a drag
never does.

ponytail: the fallback and the reorder tracker stay two small single-
purpose handlers rather than one gesture engine; no auto-scroll while
dragging (a long list moves in stages); isCoarsePointer reads the
primary pointer, so a trackpad iPad gets the touch path -- upgrade per
gesture via pointerType if that ever matters.
